Java Properties

-------------------------------

What is it:
- A .properties file is a plain text file that stores configuration data
  as key=value pairs outside of your compiled code
- Managed via java.util.Properties class (java.util.Properties)
- Lines starting with # are comments and are ignored

Example .properties file:
  db.url=localhost
  db.user=admin
  db.password=secret
  # this is a comment

-------------------------------

How to use it:
  Properties props = new Properties();

  // load from file
  props.load(new FileInputStream("config.properties"));

  // read a value
  String url = props.getProperty("db.url");

  // set a value
  props.setProperty("db.url", "newhost");

  // save back to file
  props.store(new FileOutputStream("config.properties"), "comment");

-------------------------------

Why it's useful:
- Separates configuration from code — no need to recompile when a value changes
- Common use cases: database credentials, file paths, app settings, localization strings
- Can be stored anywhere in the project, even inside a .jar file
- Easy to read and edit without touching source code

Example benefit:
  Instead of hardcoding "localhost" in 10 places across your code,
  store it once in config.properties and read it wherever needed.
  When it changes, update only the file — not the code.